<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#zenden">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Zenden</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Dizains, Seven Zenden</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Zenden (French dizains) were the self-governing communal districts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-valais">Upper Valais</a>, in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a>. Seven of them, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goms">Goms</a>, Brig, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/visp">Visp</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leuk">Leuk</a> and Sion, gradually took power from the prince-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> of Sion in the 15th to 17th centuries and forced them to give up temporal rule. Allied with the Swiss Confederacy, they governed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valais">Valais</a> as a republic and held the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-valais">Lower Valais</a> as a subject land until 1798.</p></div>
